                           store persists → subscribers re-render

  The one rule that makes this a notification system rather than an event log: [1mthe actor is excluded from their own [0m
  [1mfan-out.[0m Alice renaming a task she watches notifies Bob and Carol, never Alice. Every real system does this and it's
  the first thing that looks broken if you skip it.

  [1m4. Error handling[0m

  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m unavailable or holding corrupt JSON → fall back to seeded in-memory state and show a one-line banner.
    The demo must not die on a bad key.
  - A notification whose [38;5;153mtaskId[39m no longer exists (the delete case) → renders normally from its snapshot, but isn't
    clickable. This is the expected path, not an error.
  - Unknown [38;5;153mactorId[39m or notification [38;5;153mtype[39m → render a generic message rather than throwing.

  [1m5. Testing[0m

  [38;5;153mnotify.js[39m and [38;5;153mstore.js[39m are pure logic and get unit tests. The cases worth writing: actor excluded from their own
  fan-out; non-watchers get nothing; delete produces a notification that survives the task; unread count is per-user;
  switching users doesn't leak state between them. Views get no automated tests — manual check in the browser.

